    feat: wire pipeline StageResult/recovery/convergence outcomes into /metrics counters. Addresses OBS-2.
    
    OBS-1 gave SpecLang a Prometheus scrape surface; OBS-2 makes that surface tell
    the truth about the compile loop. The pipeline executor, the recovery executor
    and the daemon that drives them run in a DIFFERENT process from the MCP HTTP
    server that answers GET /metrics, so an in-memory counter can never show a delta
    across that boundary. The outcomes those producers compute are persisted to a
    small JSON ledger and merged into the scrape at render time.
    
    Ledger design (specs/pipeline.spec.dir/src/metrics-ledger.ts):
    - path: $SPECLANG_METRICS_LEDGER, else <cwd>/.speclang/metrics-outcomes.json, so
      a daemon and a scraper sharing a project directory share the ledger with no
      extra wiring;
    - atomic: temp file in the same directory + rename, so a scrape running
      concurrently with a producer sees the old snapshot or the new one, never a
      torn file;
    - crash tolerant: a missing, unparsable or wrongly shaped file reads as the
      empty ledger (counters clamped to finite non-negative numbers, unknown keys
      dropped) — never an exception, never a fabricated count;
    - monotonic: read-modify-write with a non-negative delta per observation, so
      counters only ever increase;
    - side-effect only: an unwritable ledger path makes the recorder return false;
      it never throws and never changes pipeline control flow. A test runs a real
      pipeline against an unwritable path and asserts the stages still complete;
    - series keys encode the label set as sorted, backslash-escaped k=v pairs, so
      the on-disk shape stays flat and diffable.
    
    Families (declared unconditionally, so a fresh install — and the acceptance
    grep — still sees HELP/TYPE before anything has run):
    speclang_pipeline_stage_results_total{stage,status},
    speclang_pipeline_recovery_total{outcome},
    speclang_pipeline_convergence_total{outcome}, and the
    speclang_pipeline_stage_duration_seconds{stage} gauge. Label values are stage
    names plus three fixed vocabularies, so cardinality stays bounded.
    
    Instrumentation sites: PipelineExecutor.execute() records every completed
    StageResult (success/failure), every condition-skipped stage, and the
    ConvergenceResult it was invoked with; RecoveryExecutor.execute() records each
    action outcome (executeAll routes through it). execute() is now a thin wrapper
    over the previous body (renamed executeAction) purely to give the recording one
    seam — the action implementations are untouched.
    
    Bare `node bin/speclang cascade` cannot demonstrate this, and it was NOT faked
    by counting help text: `cascade` is a commander command group — `speclang
    cascade` with no <spec> prints usage, and the group only declares
    status/trigger/abort. Nothing in bin/speclang, src/cli/*.ts or
    src/cli/commands/*.ts references PipelineExecutor, so no CLI subcommand reaches
    the producers. The acceptance run therefore drives the real compiled
    PipelineExecutor (the same class the daemon calls on convergence) in its own
    process against a scratch project, and reads the delta off a separately started
    MCP server: 0 -> 4 -> 8 stage results, 0 -> 2 -> 4 recovery outcomes, 0 -> 1 -> 2
    convergence outcomes, plus the stage-duration gauge. docs/observability-spec.md
    section 9 records the families, the ledger format and the exact commands.
    
    Found while running that demonstration, deliberately NOT fixed here (separate
    row): the daemon's dynamic import of the executor tries
    '../../pipeline.spec.dir/src/executor.js' and
    '../../specs/pipeline.spec.dir/src/executor.js', but the pipeline compiles to
    dist/src/pipeline/, so a real `speclang start` cascade ends with
    'Pipeline execution error ... Cannot find module
    <repo>/dist/specs/pipeline.spec.dir/src/executor.js' and the pipeline never
    runs. That pre-existing loader path bug is unchanged by this commit.
    
